														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4862716">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>People in close proximity to the mirror are mimetically compelled to want to stare into the mirror.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I would advise not doing this: forcing the mirror to be used like this kinda removes your ability to explore why someone might choose to look into the mirror; but it also removes some of the danger in that, you can't as easily have 'I caught a glimpse of the mirror in a reflect' happen say because guess what, the mirror forces you to start into it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Any sentient non-anomalous entity that stares into the mirror will undergo transfiguration based on some internal quality, desire or fear.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So like, what are these transfigurations like? Are we talking minor stuff like 'their hair colour changes' or are we talking major stuff like 'they turn into a rabbit' or what?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Most transfiguration seem to be malicious in nature, however some notable exceptions have been observed.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What do you mean by 'malicious'? Like as in 'the transfiguration is done to hurt the person being transformed' or as in 'the transfiguration turns them into a monster'?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>May be vaguely linked to SCP-352</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I wouldn't cross-link to an old series 1 SCP like this but *shrugs*.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Expands the lore of SCP's with a strong connection to specific countries (Russia)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>How does it do this? It feels like this is just by piggybacking off 352 but?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Transfiguration based SCP's aren't terribly common on the wiki. Especially ones that aren't inherently detrimental.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Earlier you said that most transfigurations 'seem to be malicious in nature' so this does feel like something that's inherently detrimental?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The Nature of the SCP also compels people to try and utilize it for good</p>
</blockquote>
<p>How does this happen? It feels like it just compels people to look into it? But there's also not really any indication of how they could use it for good at all?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I have little experience using HTML formatting and templates.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The <a href="/how-to-write-an-scp">How To Write an SCP</a> guide and <a href="/scp-style-resource">SCP Style Resource</a> include a bunch of examples of basic and advanced formatting. You can also hit &quot;+ Options&quot; at the bottom of an article and then &quot;Page Source&quot; to see how an article creates various formatting things.</p>
<hr />
<p>I have spent a lot of time so far talking about relatively minor aspects of this but I want to talk about my core concern with this: this is too much of a description of a weird magical item at the moment; it feels a lot like this is the kinda thing that I'd expect to see as a cursed item (or a trap) in a game of DnD and not like the kinda thing I'd expect to see as a SCP. The core of writing a good SCP is that what you do with a SCP is a lot more important than what a SCP can do: if you just describe the SCP, you will likely have a lot of difficulty in getting a reader to care about what happens, and even modern short SCPs that don't have long addendums work because at their core, they have an interesting thing with shocking implications. This doesn't have that, but it also doesn't really have an exploration of what the SCP does, and without giving this more narrative weight, I don't see it working.</p>
<p>There's a bunch of guides in the <a href="/comprehensive-guide-hub">Comprehensive Guide Hub</a>, particularly in the 'Misc. Essays' section at the end, that talk more about how you can tell interesting stories within the SCP format; but often the best way to learn is to read a lot of modern SCPs.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

							</div>

								
					
