			<div class="content" id="post-content-4862709">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I'm not fully convinced by this as a SCP article: like, the actual emotional beats here and the story you're telling are pretty fun but it feels like having to tell this largely from the Foundation's perspective will feel weird and like, blunten the emotional impact.</p>
<p>Indeed, the Foundation's involvement here feels really awkward as a whole: if they know this place is anomalous, it really feels like they'd do more to keep it in containment than they do, and also that they wouldn't like&#8230;just let people go there as patrons? It also feels like a Foundation site is a strange place for a bar, like, I guess they function as small towns but it feels odd that the person thinks this is going to be the most effective place to put the bar and it feels odder that they turn out to be right?</p>
<p>I guess, when it comes down to it, what I am saying is that I think this would be best as a tale from the perspective of the bartender where the Foundation are at best minor side characters, or possibly a secondary antagonist. Like, if the story was the Foundation trying to shut down the bar, and the bartender trying to do everything in his power and being forced to keep moving the bar, then it feels like you'd have a lot more effective of a story; and it also feels like it'd help explain why e.g. the bartender isn't managing to make enough money. (I would still tell this as a tale from the perspective of the bartender if you want to do that though btw).</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

